  "summary": "40,000 singers in parish dress — the world's largest choir wears its history.",
  "description": "The Latvian Song and Dance Celebration, first held in 1873, grew into a five-yearly gathering of up to 40,000 singers whose parade and stage dress have become a visual institution: region-specific national costumes (novada tērpi) with woven sashes, crowns, white blouses and wool skirts, each parish pattern load-bearing with meaning. The festival — UNESCO-listed with its Estonian and Lithuanian siblings as a Masterpiece of Oral and Intangible Heritage — fuses choral nation-building with textile archive; costume committees authenticate regional dress, and the Riga parade of folk-dress columns is the single densest display of living Baltic textile art anywhere.",
  "culturalContext": "The Song Celebration (UNESCO Masterpiece with Estonian and Lithuanian siblings) is Latvia's national sacrament — the 1873 founding fed choral nation-building, Soviet-era festivals kept identity alive, and the parade of parish-verified costumes is the densest living display of Baltic textile heritage anywhere.",
